    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '251. A pregnant woman at 38 weeks gestation suddenly develops hypotension, hypoxia, and seizures during labor, followed by cardiovascular collapse and DIC. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Severe pre-eclampsia", "Amniotic fluid embolism", "Peripartum cardiomyopathy", "Acute MI"]', 1, 'Amniotic fluid embolism is a rare but catastrophic emergency characterized by the triad of hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy (DIC). It often occurs during labor or immediately postpartum.'),
    (tid, '252. Which ECG finding is most characteristic of an acute large pulmonary embolism?', '["S1Q3T3 pattern", "Sinus tachycardia", "New RBBB", "Right axis deviation"]', 1, 'While S1Q3T3 is the "classic" board answer, sinus tachycardia is actually the most common ECG abnormality found in patients with pulmonary embolism.'),
    (tid, '253. In a patient with an inferior wall MI, ST elevation in lead V4R (right-sided lead) has a high specificity for which complication?', '["Left main occlusion", "Right ventricular infarction", "Posterior wall extension", "High lateral MI"]', 1, 'Lead V4R is the most useful lead for identifying right ventricular involvement in the setting of an inferior MI. Identifying RV MI is critical because these patients are preload-dependent and may develop severe hypotension if given nitrates.'),
    (tid, '254. A 45-year-old male with a history of IV drug use presents with a new diastolic murmur at the left sternal border and signs of acute heart failure. Which valve is likely affected?', '["Tricuspid valve", "Aortic valve", "Mitral valve", "Pulmonic valve"]', 1, 'While IV drug users often have tricuspid endocarditis, a new diastolic murmur at the left sternal border suggests aortic regurgitation, indicating aortic valve involvement. Left-sided endocarditis is more common than right-sided even in IVDU, though IVDU have a higher incidence of right-sided disease than the general population.'),
    (tid, '255. What is the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) used for?', '["To diagnose PE in high-risk patients", "To rule out PE in patients with a low clinical pre-test probability without the need for a D-dimer", "To determine the dose of thrombolytics", "To identify patients who need an IVC filter"]', 1, 'The PERC rule is applied to patients with a low clinical suspicion of PE (Wells < 2). If all 8 criteria are met, the probability of PE is < 1%, and no further testing (including D-dimer) is required.'),
    (tid, '256. A patient presents with "the worst headache of my life" and a BP of 210/120. ECG shows deep, wide, inverted T waves (Cerebral T waves). What is the underlying cause of the ECG changes?', '["Acute MI", "Subarachnoid hemorrhage", "Hyperkalemia", "Digoxin toxicity"]', 1, 'Intracranial hemorrhage, particularly subarachnoid hemorrhage, can cause dramatic ECG changes including prolonged QT intervals and large, wide, inverted T waves (Cerebral T waves) due to massive sympathetic surge.'),
    (tid, '257. What is the classic triad of cardiac tamponade (Beck''s Triad)?', '["Hypotension, Bradycardia, Muffled heart sounds", "Hypotension, JVD, Muffled heart sounds", "Hypertension, JVD, Crackles", "Hypotension, Tachycardia, Clear lungs"]', 1, 'Beck''s triad for cardiac tamponade consists of hypotension, jugular venous distension (JVD), and muffled heart sounds. It is found in only about 10-40% of cases.'),
    (tid, '258. A dialysis patient misses two sessions and presents with weakness and a heart rate of 30 bpm. ECG shows a "sine wave" pattern. What is the immediate treatment?', '["IV Atropine", "IV Calcium Gluconate or Calcium Chloride", "Transcutaneous pacing", "IV Glucagon"]', 1, 'The sine wave pattern is a late and ominous sign of severe hyperkalemia. IV calcium is the first-line treatment to stabilize the cardiac membrane and prevent arrest.'),
    (tid, '259. A 60-year-old male with a history of smoking and hypertension presents with sudden onset of severe, "tearing" chest pain radiating to the back. BP is 180/100. CXR shows a widened mediastinum. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute MI", "Aortic dissection", "Pulmonary embolism", "Esophageal rupture"]', 1, 'Tearing or ripping pain radiating to the back, combined with a widened mediastinum on CXR, is classic for aortic dissection.'),
    (tid, '260. In the treatment of aortic dissection, why is a beta-blocker (like Esmolol) typically given before a vasodilator (like Nitroprusside)?', '["To prevent reflex tachycardia and increased shear stress (dP/dt)", "To improve renal perfusion", "Because vasodilators are contraindicated", "To prevent hypotension"]', 0, 'Vasodilators can cause reflex tachycardia, which increases the shear stress (rate of rise of pressure, or dP/dt) on the aortic wall, potentially worsening the dissection. Beta-blockers reduce both heart rate and dP/dt.'),
    (tid, '261. A 30-year-old female with no past medical history presents with sharp chest pain that is worse when lying flat and better when sitting forward. ECG shows diffuse ST elevation and PR depression. Diagnosis?', '["STEMI", "Acute pericarditis", "Aortic dissection", "Pneumothorax"]', 1, 'Positional chest pain combined with diffuse ST elevation and PR depression across multiple leads is characteristic of acute pericarditis.'),
    (tid, '262. What is the drug of choice for treating a "Tet spell" in an infant with Tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Albuterol", "Morphine", "Furosemide", "Epinephrine"]', 1, 'Morphine helps calm the child and reduces the infundibular spasm that causes the hypercyanotic spell. Other maneuvers include the knee-to-chest position and oxygen.'),
    (tid, '263. A patient with a permanent pacemaker presents with a heart rate of 30 bpm. ECG shows pacer spikes that are not followed by QRS complexes. What is this called?', '["Failure to sense", "Failure to capture", "Oversensing", "Pacemaker syndrome"]', 1, 'Failure to capture occurs when the pacemaker delivers an electrical stimulus (the spike) but the myocardium fails to depolarize (no QRS).'),
    (tid, '264. What is the mechanism of action of Adenosine in treating SVT?', '["Blocks the sodium-potassium pump", "Slows conduction through the AV node", "Increases the refractory period of the ventricles", "Stimulates beta-receptors"]', 1, 'Adenosine slows conduction through the AV node and can interrupt re-entry circuits that involve the AV node.'),
    (tid, '265. A patient on Digoxin presents with nausea, blurred vision, and a "yellow-green" tint to their vision. ECG shows a regular rhythm at 40 bpm. What electrolyte abnormality increases the risk of this toxicity?', '["Hyperkalemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hypernatremia", "Hypocalcemia"]', 1, 'Hypokalemia increases the binding of Digoxin to the Na+/K+ ATPase pump, thereby increasing its toxic effects.'),
    (tid, '266. A 24-year-old male presents with palpitations. ECG shows a short PR interval and a slurred upstroke of the QRS (Delta wave). What is the name of this syndrome?', '["Brugada syndrome", "Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome", "Lown-Ganong-Levine syndrome", "Long QT syndrome"]', 1, 'WPW syndrome is characterized by an accessory pathway (Bundle of Kent) that pre-excites the ventricles, leading to the delta wave and short PR interval.'),
    (tid, '267. In a patient with WPW and Atrial Fibrillation, why is Diltiazem or Verapamil contraindicated?', '["They cause seizures", "They can paradoxically increase the ventricular rate by blocking the AV node and forcing all conduction through the accessory pathway", "They cause renal failure", "They increase the risk of bleeding"]', 1, 'Blocking the AV node in WPW with AF allows more impulses to travel down the accessory pathway, which can lead to ventricular fibrillation.'),
    (tid, '268. A patient is in ventricular fibrillation. You have delivered one shock and resumed CPR. When should the next rhythm check occur?', '["Immediately after the shock", "After 2 minutes of CPR", "After 5 minutes of CPR", "After the first dose of Epinephrine"]', 1, 'Current ACLS guidelines emphasize 2-minute cycles of high-quality CPR between rhythm checks/shocks to maximize coronary perfusion pressure.'),
    (tid, '269. What is the first-line treatment for Torsades de Pointes?', '["Amiodarone", "Magnesium Sulfate 2g IV", "Lidocaine", "Procainamide"]', 1, 'IV Magnesium Sulfate is the treatment of choice for Torsades de Pointes, even if the serum magnesium level is normal.'),
    (tid, '270. A patient presents with cardiogenic shock. Hemodynamic monitoring shows a high PCWP (Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure). What does this indicate?', '["Low volume in the left atrium", "Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure is elevated, suggesting fluid backup", "Right-sided heart failure", "Dehydration"]', 1, 'A high PCWP indicates that the "pump" is failing to move blood forward, leading to increased pressure in the left atrium and pulmonary vasculature.'),
    (tid, '271. What is the primary side effect to watch for when administering IV Nitroglycerin for hypertensive emergency?', '["Hypertension", "Headache and hypotension", "Hyperkalemia", "Bradycardia"]', 1, 'Nitroglycerin is a potent vasodilator. Headache is common due to cerebral vasodilation, and hypotension can occur if the dose is too high or titrated too quickly.'),
    (tid, '272. A 2-week-old infant presents with gray, mottled skin, weak pulses in all extremities, and severe metabolic acidosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Sepsis", "Ductal-dependent congenital heart disease (e.g., Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome)", "Pyloric stenosis", "Intussusception"]', 1, 'As the ductus arteriosus closes in the first weeks of life, infants with ductal-dependent systemic circulation (like HLHS or critical coarctation) will develop sudden cardiovascular collapse.'),
    (tid, '273. What is the definitive treatment for an infant with ductal-dependent congenital heart disease presenting in shock?', '["Epinephrine", "Prostaglandin E1 (Alprostadil) infusion", "High-dose furosemide", "Dopamine"]', 1, 'Prostaglandin E1 is used to keep the ductus arteriosus open (or re-open it), allowing for systemic perfusion until surgical correction can occur.'),
    (tid, '274. A patient with a large pericardial effusion has "Electrical Alternans" on their ECG. What does this represent?', '["Alternating heart block", "The heart swinging back and forth within the fluid-filled pericardial sac", "A malfunctioning pacemaker", "Electrolyte shifts"]', 1, 'Electrical alternans is a beat-to-beat variation in the height of the QRS complex, caused by the heart physically swinging in the effusion.'),
    (tid, '275. Which medication used in ACLS has been shown to improve the rate of ROSC (Return of Spontaneous Circulation) but NOT the rate of survival to hospital discharge with good neurological outcome?', '["Amiodarone", "Epinephrine", "Atropine", "Sodium Bicarbonate"]', 1, 'Large trials (like PARAMEDIC2) have shown that Epinephrine increases ROSC but does not significantly improve the rate of neurologically intact survival compared to placebo.');
